agents operate through natural language interfaces and can exhibit unpredictable behaviors when subjected to adversarial inputs or edge cases. The consequences of such failures can be severe, ranging from introduction of security vulnerabilities in generated code to silent misclassifications that compromise system integrity.\n\nThis paper addresses the growing need for systematic evaluation of code agent security and reliability. We focus particularly on the application of red-teaming methodologies to code agents—structured adversarial testing designed to uncover vulnerabilities and failure modes before deployment. Red-teaming has proven valuable for evaluating the safety and trustworthiness of large language models, but its application to code-specific agents presents unique challenges due to the executable nature of code and the complex dependencies involved in software systems.\n\nOur contributions include: (1) a comprehensive survey of code agent architectures and capabilities; (2) an analysis of attack vectors specific to code-generating agents; (3) a proposed red-teaming framework tailored for code agents; and (4) discussion of evaluation metrics and best practices for assessing code agent trustworthiness.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents has evolved rapidly, building upon decades of research in automated programming and more recent advances in neural code generation. Early work on automated programming focused on program synthesis and template-based code generation, while contemporary approaches leverage large language models trained on vast code corpora.\n\nThe trustworthiness and safety of large language models has become a major research focus. Wang et al. [1] presented DecodingTrust, which provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models across multiple dimensions including harmful content, biases, factual consistency, and robustness. Their work established evaluation frameworks that have influenced how we think about assessing AI system safety, providing methodological foundations that can be adapted for code agent evaluation.\n\nThe deep learning revolution that enabled modern code agents was fundamentally enabled by architectural innovations in neural networks. He et al. [2] introduced residual learning for image recognition, demonstrating that skip connections could enable training of substantially deeper networks. This breakthrough in residual learning became foundational for later transformer architectures, which form the backbone of modern code agents. Without these advances in deep learning architecture, the capabilities of contemporary code agents would not be possible.\n\nResearch on adversarial robustness of neural networks has also informed red-teaming approaches. Studies have shown that neural networks can be vulnerable to carefully crafted adversarial examples that cause incorrect predictions while being imperceptible to human observers. These findings have motivated the development of red-teaming protocols that systematically probe models for vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the specific domain of code agents, recent work has explored vulnerabilities in LLM-based code generation. Studies have demonstrated that code agents can be manipulated to generate insecure code, introduce vulnerabilities, or execute unintended actions when prompted with adversarial inputs. These findings underscore the need for dedicated red-teaming methodologies for code agents.\n\n[1] Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[2] He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., and Sun, J. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n## Code Agent Architectures and Capabilities\n\nModern code agents typically combine several components: a language model backbone for code understanding and generation, external tools for code execution and testing, and specialized prompting mechanisms for task decomposition. The most capable agents employ multi-step reasoning, allowing them to break down complex programming tasks into manageable subtasks.\n\nCode generation capabilities have improved dramatically with the scaling of training data and model parameters. Modern code agents can write functions, classes, and even entire programs from natural language specifications. They can also perform code completion, bug fixing, and code refactoring with increasing accuracy.\n\nBeyond generation, code agents are increasingly equipped with execution capabilities. By running generated code in sandboxed environments, agents can test their own outputs, debug errors, and iterate on solutions. This self-correction capability significantly improves the quality and reliability of generated code.\n\n## Attack Vectors and Vulnerabilities\n\nCode agents face several categories of attack vectors that can be exploited by adversarial actors:\n\n### Prompt Injection Attacks\nAdversaries can craft inputs that bypass safety filters or manipulate the agent's behavior. In the context of code agents, this might involve injecting malicious instructions that cause the agent to generate vulnerable code or execute harmful operations.\n\n### Context Poisoning\nSince code agents often operate within the context of existing codebases, adversaries can introduce subtle vulnerabilities into the context that the agent then propagates or amplifies. This is analogous to data poisoning attacks in machine learning.\n\n...\n112 /app/paper.txt\n",
